  q(MW,'Flushing, headache and peppery taste soon after eating tuna suggests which illness?','Scombroid poisoning',['Infant botulism','Ciguatera poisoning','Hepatitis A'],'D','Histamine forms when fish is not kept cold; cooking does not destroy it.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Ciguatera fish poisoning is linked to eating which type of fish?','Large reef fish',['Farmed catfish','Canned sardines','Smoked salmon'],'A','Toxins from algae build up in reef fish such as barracuda; symptoms include reversed hot-cold sensation.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Why does Cryptosporidium cause outbreaks at chlorinated swimming pools?','It resists chlorine',['It grows on pool tiles','It spreads by mosquitoes','It is killed by sunlight'],'B','Crypto can survive days in properly chlorinated water; swimmers with diarrhea should stay out.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome is linked to exposure to:','Rodent droppings',['Bird feathers','Tick bites','Raw shellfish'],'C','People breathe in the virus when cleaning areas contaminated by deer mice.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'For how long after rash onset should a person with measles be isolated?','Four days',['One hour','Two months','One year'],'D','People with measles are contagious from four days before to four days after the rash appears.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Which tests screen for latent tuberculosis infection?','TST or IGRA',['PCR or ELISA','CBC or CMP','ESR or CRP'],'A','The tuberculin skin test or an interferon-gamma release assay detects infection; a chest X-ray checks for disease.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'In the 2022 mpox outbreak, how was the virus mainly spread?','Close skin-to-skin contact',['Contaminated drinking water','Aedes mosquito bites','Undercooked meat'],'B','The outbreak spread mostly through intimate contact; vaccination and awareness helped control it.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'In a fully susceptible population, about how many people does one measles case infect?','12 to 18',['1 to 2','3 to 5','50 to 60'],'C','This high R₀ means about 95% of people must be immune to prevent outbreaks.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Valley fever (coccidioidomycosis) is endemic in which part of the United States?','The Southwest',['New England','The Great Lakes','Hawaii'],'D','People breathe in fungal spores from desert soil, especially during dust storms or digging.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Where in the United States is dengue endemic?','Puerto Rico',['Alaska','Maine','North Dakota'],'A','Aedes mosquitoes spread dengue there; local cases also occur at times in Florida and Texas.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'In health care infection reports, what does CLABSI stand for?','Central line-associated bloodstream infection',['Chronic lung airway blockage syndrome','Clinical lab antibiotic sensitivity index','Contact-linked airborne spread incident'],'B','CLABSIs are tracked by the CDC’s National Healthcare Safety Network and are often preventable.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'What is an MMWR “QuickStats” feature?','A one-chart national data brief',['A weekly list of product recalls','A summary of new health laws','A practice quiz for clinicians'],'C','QuickStats often present data from the National Center for Health Statistics in one graph.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'The CDC’s blood lead reference value for children is:','3.5 µg/dL',['0.5 µg/dL','10 µg/dL','45 µg/dL'],'D','The value was lowered from 5 to 3.5 µg/dL in 2021; no safe blood lead level has been found.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'What does the CDC’s National Immunization Survey measure?','Vaccination coverage',['Vaccine side effects','Vaccine prices','Vaccine storage'],'A','The survey estimates coverage among children and teens by state.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'An odds ratio of 1.0 means:','No association with disease',['Exposure doubles the odds','Exposure prevents disease','Every case was exposed'],'B','An OR above 1 suggests a risk factor; below 1 suggests a protective factor.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'A risk ratio’s 95% confidence interval is 0.8 to 2.4. What does this suggest?','The result is not statistically significant',['The exposure clearly causes disease','The exposure clearly prevents disease','The study had no bias at all'],'C','Because the interval includes 1.0, chance alone could explain the difference.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'A study reports p < 0.05. What does this usually mean?','Chance alone is an unlikely cause',['The effect is large and important','The study has no confounding','The finding is proven true'],'D','Statistical significance does not show that an effect is large or causal.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'Case-control studies select participants based on:','Disease status',['Exposure status','Random assignment','Treatment group'],'A','Investigators start with people who have the disease and compare their past exposures with controls.',MMWR),
  q(MW,'In a foodborne outbreak, a food-specific attack rate table compares:','Illness in eaters vs. non-eaters',['Food prices at each event','Calories in each menu item','Cooking times for each dish'],'B','The food with a high attack rate among eaters and a low rate among non-eaters is the likely source.',MMWR)
